I'd first check whether the loop's iterations are actually independent — if fetching record N doesn't need anything from record N-1's result, the `await`-in-a-loop pattern is almost certainly the bug, because it serializes requests that could run in parallel.

```javascript
// Serial: each request waits for the previous to fully finish
for (const id of ids) {
  const record = await fetchRecord(id); // 150ms x 10 = ~1500ms
  results.push(record);
}

// Parallel: all requests fire together
const results = await Promise.all(ids.map((id) => fetchRecord(id))); // ~150ms total
```

The root cause: each `await` inside the loop body fully suspends that iteration until its promise settles *before the next iteration even starts*, turning what should be one 150ms round trip (times whatever concurrency the server can handle) into ten sequential 150ms round trips.

The fix is to separate **starting** the requests from **waiting** on them: map over the ids without awaiting inside the callback, producing an array of already-in-flight promises, then `await Promise.all(...)` once at the end. I'd also flag one caveat: this is only safe if the downstream service can actually handle ten concurrent requests — for a rate-limited API, unlimited parallelism just trades one problem (slowness) for another (429s), so a batched approach with a concurrency limit (e.g. `p-limit`) is the more careful production answer.

**Testing:** whether you can both spot this exact anti-pattern instantly and reason about the real-world limits of the naive parallel fix.
